      "s6-c4-fixed-variable",
      "Fixed and Variable Annuities",
      "An annuity is an investment contract between a person and an insurance company. Its main purpose is to save for retirement and possibly give lifetime income in retirement.\n\nThe owner puts money in, either as a lump sum or in regular payments. Any growth is tax-deferred, which means no tax is due until money comes out. At retirement, the owner can start taking payments monthly, in a lump sum, or now and then. Or the owner can annuitize, which means turning the contract into regular payments, usually for life.\n\nThere are two basic types.\n\nFixed annuity: The insurance company puts the premiums in its general account and guarantees a specific rate of return. A fixed annuity is not a security, because the insurer picks the investments and takes the investment risk. Fixed annuities are insurance products regulated only by state insurance laws. If the owner annuitizes, the insurer guarantees fixed payments, usually for life. The owner takes no investment risk and knows each payment amount. The risk is that fixed payments may not keep up with inflation. So fixed annuities carry purchasing power risk (inflation risk).\n\nVariable annuity: The payments go into a separate account. This account is kept apart from the insurance company's general account. Inside it, the investor picks among subaccounts, which are mutual funds or UITs holding mutual funds. There is generally no guaranteed rate of return. The customer bears the investment risk. The annuity's value rises and falls with its investments. If the customer annuitizes, each payment also varies. That is why it is called a variable annuity.\n\nVariable annuities are regulated by state insurance laws, like fixed annuities. But they are also securities, so they are regulated by federal and state securities laws too. They must be sold with a prospectus. Anyone who sells an annuity needs a state insurance license. Sellers of variable products must also be registered with FINRA to sell securities, such as with the Series 6 or Series 7.",
